)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381641 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381642 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381643 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186381644 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1041 "field-1" 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1104 #edb/ref 17592186381644 13194139536061 true] [17592186381640 1110 #uuid "76115cd8-b45d-59ac-95e3-85798f55de7c" 13194139536061 true] [17592186381641 1034 "n=4" 13194139536061 true] [17592186381641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536061 true] [17592186381641 1111 #uuid "ba2ec886-54bf-5294-b6a0-d9fe9bad7746" 13194139536061 true] [17592186381642 1034 "n=1" 13194139536061 true] [17592186381642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536061 tr